            <ArticleTitle>Discovery and pharmacological effects of a novel GPR142 antagonist.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>290-296</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/10799893.2016.1247861</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>GPR142 is a G-protein-coupled receptor (GPCR), whose most potent and efficacious ligand has been reported as being the natural amino acid l-tryptophan. GPR142 is highly expressed in pancreatic β-cells and immune cells, suggesting the receptor may play a role in the pathogenesis and development of diabetes or inflammatory diseases. In a previous report, we developed GPR142 agonists as insulin secretagogues. In this report, we show the discovery of a selective, potent small-molecule GPR142 antagonist, CLP-3094, and its pharmacological characteristics. These data support targeting this receptor for the treatment of chronic inflammatory diseases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
